        <body>
            <div xml:id="d157">

                <pb facs="0392" n="350" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v27/pg350"/>

                <head>157. Memorandum From James C. Thomson, Jr., of the National Security Council
                    Staff to the Presidentʼs Special Assistant for National Security Affairs
                        (Bundy)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d157-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1965-08-16">August 16, 1965</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>The Week in Asia</item>
                </list>
                <p>[Here follows information on Vietnam, Burma, and Japan.]</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Cambodia</hi>
                </p>
                <p>[<hi rendition="#italic">less than 1 line of source text not declassified</hi>]
                    has accidentally uncovered anti-Sihanouk Khmer Serei leader Son Ngoc Thanh
                    somewhere in Saigon, now wants to establish a dialogue. State is properly
                    skittish as this guy has the political future of Harold Stassen but drives
                    Sihanouk crazy. We are permitting one or two more “contacts” with him—but we may
                    be risking a further Cambodia blow-up (our relations with Sihanouk are currently
                    euphoric relative to the recent past).</p>
                <p>[Here follows information on Taiwan and Singapore.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#bold">T</hi>
                        <anchor n="2" xml:id="d157-f2"/> RC </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
